= System boundary — Simple =

A '''system boundary''' is the line we use to decide what is inside a system and what is outside it.

Sometimes that line is physical. A cell membrane separates a cell from its surroundings.

But many system boundaries are chosen for a particular question.

Imagine a website stops working. If we define the system as only the website's own code, a failed cloud service may look like an outside problem. If we define the whole product—including the cloud service—as the system, the same failure becomes part of the system we are responsible for designing.

Changing the boundary can therefore change:

* which parts we study;
* which causes we notice;
* which people or organizations are included;
* what we measure;
* who seems responsible for a result.

A good analysis says what its boundary is instead of pretending that the boundary is always obvious.

